## Solution

We evaluate
$$L = \lim_{n \to \infty} \left[\Phi(x\ln n)\right]^n, \qquad x > 0,$$
where $\Phi(t) = \int_{-\infty}^{t} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-y^2/2}\,dy$ is the standard normal CDF.

### Setup

Set $t = x\ln n$, so $n = e^{t/x}$ and $t \to \infty$ as $n\to\infty$. Then
$$L = \lim_{t\to\infty} \Phi(t)^{e^{t/x}}.$$

Taking logarithms, it suffices to compute
$$\lim_{t\to\infty} e^{t/x}\,\ln\Phi(t).$$

### Tail asymptotics of the normal

For large $t$, the well-known asymptotic (obtained via integration by parts on the tail) gives
$$1 - \Phi(t) = \int_t^\infty \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-y^2/2}\,dy \sim \frac{1}{t\sqrt{2\pi}}\,e^{-t^2/2}.$$

Since $1 - \Phi(t) \to 0$, we use $\ln(1-u) \sim -u$ for $u\to 0^+$:
$$\ln\Phi(t) = \ln\!\big(1-(1-\Phi(t))\big) \sim -(1-\Phi(t)) \sim -\frac{1}{t\sqrt{2\pi}}\,e^{-t^2/2}.$$

### Combining

Therefore
$$e^{t/x}\,\ln\Phi(t) \sim -\frac{1}{t\sqrt{2\pi}}\,\exp\!\left(\frac{t}{x} - \frac{t^2}{2}\right).$$

Complete the square in the exponent:
$$\frac{t}{x} - \frac{t^2}{2} = -\frac{1}{2}\!\left(t - \frac{1}{x}\right)^{\!2} + \frac{1}{2x^2}.$$

As $t \to \infty$, the term $-\frac{1}{2}(t-1/x)^2 \to -\infty$, so
$$\exp\!\left(\frac{t}{x}-\frac{t^2}{2}\right) = \exp\!\left(\frac{1}{2x^2}\right)\cdot \exp\!\left(-\frac{1}{2}\!\left(t-\frac{1}{x}\right)^{\!2}\right) \to 0,$$

with the decay being super-exponential (Gaussian in $t$), which dominates the polynomial factor $1/t$. Hence
$$\lim_{t\to\infty} e^{t/x}\,\ln\Phi(t) = 0.$$

### Conclusion

$$L = \exp\!\left(\lim_{t\to\infty} e^{t/x}\,\ln\Phi(t)\right) = e^0 = 1.$$

This holds for every $x > 0$, since the $-t^2/2$ term always dominates $t/x$ as $t\to\infty$.

$$\boxed{\lim_{n \to \infty} \left[\int_{-\infty}^{x\ln n} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\,e^{-y^2/2}\,dy\right]^n = 1}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
